---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getApp, COMPANY } from '../../data/apps';

const app = getApp('tare')!;
const lastUpdated = 'August 5, 2026';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${app.name} Privacy Policy — ${COMPANY.name}`}
  description={`How ${app.name} handles your health information.`}
  path={`/${app.slug}/privacy`}
>
  <article class="prose container">
    <a class="back-link" href="/tare">← {app.name}</a>
    <h1>{app.name} Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="updated">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</p>

    <p>
      This policy explains how {COMPANY.name} (“we”, “us”) handles information in
      {app.name} for iOS. {app.name} is a GLP-1 and metabolic-health tracker, so
      most of what you enter is health information — some of the most personal
      information there is. This policy is written to be specific about where it
      goes rather than reassuring about it.
    </p>

    <p class="callout">
      <strong>The short version.</strong> {app.name} has no account and no
      sign-in. Your health data is stored on your device, and in your own
      private iCloud account if you have iCloud on — we cannot read either one.
      Nothing is sent to us unless you turn on the optional AI features, and if
      you do, only the specific figures named on the consent screen are sent,
      only when you ask a question, and nothing about them is stored afterwards.
      There is no advertising, no analytics and no tracking of any kind.
    </p>

    <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
    <p>
      Everything you log — weights, blood-glucose readings, injections and doses,
      meals and macros, well-being and mood, side effects, body measurements,
      lab results, vitals, diagnoses, medications, procedures, progress photos
      and meal photos — is written to a database on your iPhone.
    </p>
    <p>
      If iCloud is enabled on your device, that database also syncs through
      <strong>your own private iCloud account</strong> so your data is available
      on your other devices and survives a lost phone. That data lives in your
      Apple account, under Apple’s encryption, and we have no access to it. We
      operate no server that holds your health records, and we could not produce
      a copy of your data if we were asked to.
    </p>

    <h2>What leaves your device, and only if you turn it on</h2>
    <p>
      {app.name} includes optional AI features — the analyst that answers
      questions about your data, the summaries on the Insights and Doctor Report
      screens, meal estimates from a name or a photo, lab-document import, and
      the knowledge base.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>These are off until you turn them on.</strong> The first time you
      use one, {app.name} shows a screen naming exactly which fields would be
      sent, and nothing is sent unless you accept it. You can turn it back off
      at any time in Settings → Privacy, and if we ever change what would be
      sent, we ask again rather than relying on the answer you gave to a
      different question.
    </p>
    <p>When the features are on, and only when you ask for something:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The relevant figures for that request are sent over an encrypted
        connection to a small relay service we operate.
      </li>
      <li>
        For questions about your own records, the assistant does not receive
        your database. It writes a query, your device runs that query against
        its own copy, and only the rows that come back — the specific figures
        needed to answer what you asked — are returned to it. There is no bulk
        upload and no export.
      </li>
      <li>
        The relay forwards them to <strong>Anthropic</strong>, which generates
        the answer, and returns it to your device.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The relay does not store your request or the answer.</strong> It
        keeps no database and writes no copy of them; it records error messages
        only, which do not contain your data.
      </li>
      <li>
        Anthropic processes the request under its commercial terms and does not
        use it to train models. Anthropic may retain inputs for a limited period
        for safety and abuse-prevention purposes, which is why the App Store
        privacy label for {app.name} lists health data as collected even though
        we ourselves keep none of it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      To stop the relay being abused by anyone other than the app, it verifies
      that requests come from a genuine copy of {app.name} using Apple’s App
      Attest. That checks the <em>app</em>, not you. It is not an account, it
      carries no name or email, and it is not used to build any profile.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some things are never sent, whatever you have turned on: your profile
      details, your progress photos, and any meal photo you have not
      specifically chosen to analyse.
    </p>

    <h2>Apple Health</h2>
    <p>
      If you allow it, {app.name} can read weight, glucose, sleep and step data
      from Apple Health so you do not have to enter it twice, and write back the
      weights and glucose readings you log. Nothing else is written — your
      doses, meals, well-being check-ins and medical records stay inside
      {app.name}. That exchange is between {app.name} and Health on your own
      device, and it happens when you ask for it rather than in the background.
      We never receive it, and Health data is not included in anything sent to
      the AI features unless it is also a record you logged in {app.name}.
    </p>

    <h2>Widgets, complications and the Apple Watch</h2>
    <p>
      To draw a widget or a watch complication, iOS runs a separate small
      program that cannot read {app.name}’s database. So {app.name} writes a
      short summary — the few figures those faces actually show, such as your
      latest weight and glucose and the days until your next dose — into a
      shared container on your device that only {app.name} and its own
      extensions can open. It contains no history and no records, and it never
      leaves the device.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Apple Watch app receives that same summary from your iPhone directly,
      over Apple’s device-to-device link. It does not use iCloud and does not
      talk to any server. When you log something on the watch, the entry is held
      on the watch and handed to the iPhone, which is what actually records it.
    </p>

    <h2>Backups and export</h2>
    <p>
      {app.name} can write a complete backup of your data to a file, and restore
      from one. Those files are created on your device and go wherever you choose
      to put them. If you save one to a third-party service, that service’s
      privacy policy applies to it, not this one.
    </p>

    <h2>What we do not collect</h2>
    <p>
      No advertising identifiers, no location, no contacts, no analytics, no
      crash reporting, no usage tracking, and no third-party software
      development kits of any kind. {app.name} does not track you across apps or
      websites, and there is nothing in it that could.
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping and deleting your data</h2>
    <p>
      Because we hold nothing, there is no account to close and no deletion
      request to make. Deleting the app removes the data on that device;
      deleting {app.name}’s iCloud data in iOS Settings removes the synced copy.
      You can export everything to a file first if you want to keep it.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you have a question about any of this, or believe we hold something we
      have said we do not, email
      <a href={`mailto:${COMPANY.privacyEmail}`}>{COMPANY.privacyEmail}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Children’s privacy</h2>
    <p>
      {app.name} is intended for a general, adult audience and is not directed to
      children. We do not knowingly collect personal information from children.
    </p>

    <h2>Not medical advice</h2>
    <p>
      {app.name} helps you record and review your own health information,
      including optional AI-generated summaries. Those summaries are generated by
      software and can be wrong. {app.name} does not provide medical advice,
      diagnosis or treatment, and will not tell you what dose to take. Always
      consult a qualified healthcare professional about your care.
    </p>

    <h2>The earlier web version</h2>
    <p>
      {app.name} began as a web application that used a Microsoft account to sign
      in and stored data in a private per-user database hosted on Microsoft
      Azure. That version is being retired in favour of the iOS app described
      above. While it remains available, the health data you entered there is
      held in that per-user database, encrypted in transit, and you may request
      access to or deletion of it at
      <a href={`mailto:${COMPANY.privacyEmail}`}>{COMPANY.privacyEmail}</a>. It
      is not connected to the iOS app, which keeps its own copy on your device.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      We may update this policy from time to time. Material changes are reflected
      by updating the “Last updated” date above, and a change to what leaves your
      device also re-triggers the consent screen inside the app.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about this policy or your data? Email
      <a href={`mailto:${COMPANY.privacyEmail}`}>{COMPANY.privacyEmail}</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
